# Target C-II — complement O–O trains consume non-dominant rhombi

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only low-`t` Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited two-bin dominant-orbit theorem; hostile-audited joint endpoint retention; audited Type-II interface collapse; audited stopped-train primitives; audited Section 157 two-sided O–O bracing  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current two-bin/shared-retention endpoint forces a large retained population of
complement outer-to-outer stopped trains.  The existing scalar argument charges only
their two terminal edge energies.  It does not use the length of the train between
those terminals.

That length gives a much stronger obstruction.  Every step of a stopped-train midpoint
path crosses one strict unit rhombus and has Euclidean length exactly one.  On the
other hand, a complement O–O train whose two host loaded-hull normals are separated by
angle `w` has terminal midpoints at distance at least

\[
D\sin(w/2)-d_{out}-o(D).
\]

Section 157 already charges the same train terminal directional energy at least

\[
2-2\sin(w/2)-o(1).
\]

Thus train length and terminal energy satisfy a linear tradeoff.  Summed over the
retained complement population, this creates a quadratic demand for strict rhombi.
Near the current low-`t` endpoint, however, the two-bin atomic theorem says essentially
all strict rhombi already belong to the dominant exact `pi/3` orbit.  The two demands
are incompatible.

A conservative version of this argument, discarding a favorable energy term, raises
the safe proof-only floor from `1.83287` to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.84209.
}
\]

The scalar crossing of the conservative inequality is

\[
1.842097329359247\ldots .
\]

Only the rounded value `1.84209` is claimed here.  A sharper joint incidence/energy
optimization is left to a companion refinement.

---

## 1. Current low-`t` variables

Pass to a convergent Type-II subsequence and write

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050,
\]

and

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\frac\kappa2.
\tag{RI.1}
\]

The current network envelope gives

\[
C_0\le C={T-q/2\over D}\le U,
\tag{RI.2}
\]

hence

\[
2\delta+\frac\kappa2
\le R_{50}(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
\tag{RI.3}

The hostile-audited two-bin theorem defines

\[
m_2(U):={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2}
\tag{RI.4}
\]

on the present range and gives the dominant exact-orbit train-mass floor

\[
M\ge m_2(U)-o(1).
\tag{RI.5}

It also gives the complement complete-train ceiling

\[
\boxed{
N_c\le N_2(U):=
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-m_2(U).
}
\tag{RI.6}

The hostile-audited joint routewise-retention theorem then supplies retained dominant
outer-occurrence mass `z` and retained complement O–O pair mass `p` satisfying

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge S:=\pi-N_2(U)-o(1).}
\tag{RI.7}

The dominant retained occurrences alone pay outer directional energy

\[
\boxed{E_{dom}\ge F(z)D-o(D),
\qquad F(z):=z-6\sin(z/6).}
\tag{RI.8}

---

## 2. One O–O train: midpoint length versus terminal energy

Let `tau` be one retained complement O–O train.  Its two terminal outer-edge
occurrences lie on distinguished Section-63 routes `W_i,W_j`.  Write

\[
\ell_r=s_r+d_r
\]

for route length, loaded-hull side length, and route detour.

Let `x_r` be the midpoint of the terminal contact edge on `W_r`.  The two path lengths
along `W_r` from `x_r` to its loaded route endpoints sum to `ell_r`.  Hence one loaded
endpoint `b_r` satisfies

\[
|x_r-b_r|\le {\ell_r\over2}
\le {s_{max}(F)+d_{out}\over2}.
\]

Audited Type-II interface collapse gives

\[
s_{max}(F)=o(D),
\]

so uniformly over all outer terminal occurrences,

\[
\boxed{|x_r-b_r|\le {\delta D\over2}+o(D).}
\tag{RI.9}

Let `w_tau in[0,pi]` be the smaller angular separation of the two loaded-side normal
locations used by the complement-bracing theorem.  Type-II near-disk convergence and
`s_max(F)=o(D)` imply that the chosen loaded endpoints `b_i,b_j` have the same normal
angles up to `o(1)`.  Therefore

\[
|b_i-b_j|
=D\sin(w_\tau/2)+o(D).
\tag{RI.10}

Combining (RI.9)--(RI.10),

\[
\boxed{
|x_i-x_j|
\ge D\bigl(\sin(w_\tau/2)-\delta\bigr)-o(D).
}
\tag{RI.11}

Now let `r_tau` be the number of strict rhombi traversed by the stopped train.  Between
opposite parallel sides of one unit rhombus, the corresponding midpoint displacement
is exactly the other unit side vector.  Hence every train midpoint step has length one.
The midpoint path has `r_tau` such steps, so

\[
r_\tau\ge|x_i-x_j|.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge\sin(w_\tau/2)-\delta-o(1).
}
\tag{RI.12}

This statement also covers a singleton O–O train: then `r_tau=0` and the two terminal
occurrences have the same physical midpoint, forcing their host normals to be close
unless the global detour is large.

Section 157 and the complement-bracing theorem give a terminal directional-energy
lower bound for the same train.  If `e_tau` denotes the sum of the exact directional
energies of its two terminal outer-edge occurrences, then

\[
e_\tau
\ge2-2\sin(w_\tau/2)-o(1).
\tag{RI.13}

Therefore

\[
\sin(w_\tau/2)
\ge1-{e_\tau\over2}-o(1),
\]

and (RI.12) becomes the train-length/terminal-energy tradeoff

\[
\boxed{
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{e_\tau\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{RI.14}

---

## 3. Weighted complement incidence demand

The shared-retention theorem assigns each retained complement O–O pair a weight
`a_tau in[0,1]`.  Normalize so

\[
{1\over D}\sum_\tau a_\tau\to p.
\]

Put

\[
e_c:={1\over D}\sum_\tau a_\tau e_\tau.
\]

Multiply (RI.14) by `a_tau` and sum.  Uniformity of the Type-II `o(1)` terms gives

\[
\boxed{
{1\over D^2}\sum_\tau a_\tau r_\tau
\ge p(1-\delta)-{e_c\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{RI.15}

Every strict rhombus contributes at most two weighted train incidences to the left
side, because it is the crossing of exactly two complete stopped trains and each
retained train weight is at most one.

---

## 4. Two-bin concentration leaves few rhombi for complement trains

Let the exact atomic orbit masses be `M_r`, with total atomic mass `A`, and choose the
dominant orbit.  The two-bin theorem gives

\[
M\ge m_2(A).
\]

Hence the residual exact-orbit mass is at most

\[
A-M\le A-m_2(A).
\]

The function `A-m_2(A)` is increasing on the current interval, while

\[
A\le C\le U.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
A-M\le r_2(U):=U-m_2(U).
}
\tag{RI.16}

The asymptotic strict-rhombus crossing measure decomposes by exact `pi/3` orbits.
Crossing mass in all non-dominant orbits is at most

\[
{1\over4}\sum_{r\ne *}M_r^2
\le {1\over4}(A-M)^2
\le {r_2(U)^2\over4}.
\tag{RI.17}

After freezing disjoint shrinking windows around the dominant orbit, any strict rhombus
incident to a retained complement train is not a dominant/dominant crossing.  Crossings
between a dominant window and its exterior that survive at quadratic density would
limit to a `pi/3`-related atomic phase and hence belong to the same exact orbit; such
mass is absorbed by the windows.  Thus the finite complement-incidence rhombi obey

\[
\boxed{
{R_{comp}\over D^2}
\le {r_2(U)^2\over4}+o(1).
}
\tag{RI.18}

Since the weighted incidence sum in (RI.15) is at most `2R_comp`,

\[
\boxed{
p(1-\delta)-{e_c\over2}
\le {r_2(U)^2\over2}.}
\tag{RI.19}

This is the new quadratic occupancy constraint.

---

## 5. Coarse complement-pair ceiling

The dominant retained occurrences and complement terminal occurrences belong to
disjoint train classes, so their exact outer-edge energy payments are disjoint.  From
(RI.8),

\[
e_c\le\delta-F(z)+o(1)\le\delta+o(1).
\tag{RI.20}

Discard the favorable `F(z)` term for the conservative theorem.  Equations
(RI.19)--(RI.20) give

\[
\boxed{
p\le
P(\delta,U):=
{\delta+r_2(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1).}
\tag{RI.21}

Together with (RI.7),

\[
\boxed{
z\ge
Z(t,\delta,\kappa):=
\pi-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+m_2(U)-P(\delta,U)-o(1).}
\tag{RI.22}

The dominant energy (RI.8) therefore forces the necessary condition

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge F(Z(t,\delta,\kappa)).}
\tag{RI.23}

---

## 6. The full feasible triangle again reduces to one endpoint

Write

\[
r(U):=U-m_2(U).
\]

Then

\[
S=\pi-{t+\kappa\over2}-r(U),
\qquad
P={\delta+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)},
\qquad
Z=S-P.
\]

On the interval `1.83287<=t<=1.84210`, every feasible point has

\[
C_0\le U<2.007,
\qquad
0\le\delta<0.049,
\]

and the companion certificate verifies the conservative bounds

\[
-0.06<m_2'(U)<0,
\qquad
0<r(U)<0.11.
\tag{RI.24}

### `kappa` direction

At fixed `t,delta`, `partial U/partial kappa=-1/2`.  Since `m_2'<0`,
`S` strictly increases with `kappa`; since `r` increases with `U`, `P` strictly
decreases with `kappa`.  Hence `Z` strictly increases with `kappa`.  The contradiction
margin `F(Z)-delta` is weakest at

\[
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
\tag{RI.25}

### `delta` direction

At fixed `t,kappa`,

\[
{\partial S\over\partial\delta}=2(1-m_2'(U))<2.12.
\]

Also

\[
{\partial P\over\partial\delta}
={1+r^2\over2(1-\delta)^2}
-{2r(1-m_2'(U))\over1-\delta}.
\]

Using (RI.24),

\[
{\partial Z\over\partial\delta}<2.37.
\tag{RI.26}

Throughout the same interval `0<Z<pi`, so

\[
F'(Z)\le F'(\pi)=1-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}<0.134.
\]

Therefore

\[
{\partial\over\partial\delta}[F(Z)-\delta]
<2.37(0.134)-1<0.
\tag{RI.27}

Thus maximal detour is again the weakest point.  With (RI.25) and the network boundary,

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R_{50}(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_0.
}
\tag{RI.28}

---

## 7. One-variable endpoint and certified floor

At (RI.28), put

\[
M_0=m_2(C_0),
\qquad
r_0=C_0-M_0,
\]

\[
\delta_0(t)={1\over2}\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0\right),
\]

\[
S_0(t)=\pi-C_0-{t\over2}+M_0,
\]

\[
P_0(t)={\delta_0(t)+r_0^2\over2(1-\delta_0(t))},
\qquad
Z_0(t)=S_0(t)-P_0(t).
\tag{RI.29}

A survivor requires

\[
H_0(t):=F(Z_0(t))-\delta_0(t)\le0.
\tag{RI.30}

The derivative argument above makes `H_0` strictly decreasing on the stated interval.
The Decimal certificate gives

\[
H_0(1.84209)>1.7\times10^{-5},
\]

\[
H_0(1.84210)<-6.3\times10^{-6},
\]

and brackets the scalar zero at

\[
1.842097329359247\ldots .
\tag{RI.31}

Therefore:

### Theorem RI.1 — complement rhombus-incidence floor

Every full-core Type-II survivor sequence in the stated proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge1.84209.}
\tag{RI.32}

This improves the hostile-audited two-bin shared-retention floor `1.83287` by more than
`0.0092`.

At the old endpoint `t=1.83287`, the conservative incidence ceiling is already only
about `0.0142D` retained complement O–O trains, compared with the `0.5792D` complement
mass preferred by the energy-only optimizer.  This is why the gain is macroscopic.

---

## 8. Remaining slack and next refinement

The theorem intentionally throws away two favorable facts:

1. the exact complement-energy budget is
   \[
   e_c\le\delta-F(z),
   \]
   not merely `e_c<=delta`; and
2. complement O–O pairs themselves pay the convex terminal-energy floor `J(p)`.

Keeping both terms leads to the stronger coupled system

\[
F(z)+J(p)\le\delta,
\]

\[
p(1-\delta)-{\delta-F(z)\over2}
\le {r_2(U)^2\over2},
\]

\[
z+p\ge S.
\]

The conservative theorem above is committed separately so that the geometric
train-length lemma and the quadratic dominant/complement crossing competition can be
audited independently before optimizing this sharper system.

C-II remains open.
